WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.280
Identical bacterial cultures grow, then meet the same bacterial virus, called a

2
00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:10.540
phage. Why do some plates show no survivors while another shows a jackpot?

3
00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:15.600
Each tube began alike, and every plate receives the same phage.

4
00:00:18.933 --> 00:00:22.033
Every culture starts from the same bacterial strain.

5
00:00:23.253 --> 00:00:26.253
They receive the same food and grow to similar sizes.

6
00:00:27.613 --> 00:00:29.853
The selecting phage has not arrived yet.

7
00:00:31.133 --> 00:00:33.913
The growing branches record who descended from whom.

8
00:00:34.967 --> 00:00:40.067
During one division, a heritable DNA sequence change appears in one cell.

9
00:00:41.327 --> 00:00:45.187
That cell and its descendants carry the variant as the tree keeps growing.

10
00:00:46.407 --> 00:00:49.927
The changed cell cannot know whether that variant will later help.

11
00:00:51.147 --> 00:00:53.207
The change merely enters the population.

12
00:00:54.450 --> 00:00:56.550
Timing changes the family size.

13
00:00:57.730 --> 00:01:00.290
An early event leaves a large yellow branch.

14
00:01:01.450 --> 00:01:04.050
A late event leaves only a few yellow tips.

15
00:01:05.310 --> 00:01:07.330
Some cultures get no event at all.

16
00:01:08.490 --> 00:01:13.090
Outcomes depend on whether a resistance mutation occurred and, if so, when.

17
00:01:14.133 --> 00:01:16.293
Another explanation was possible.

18
00:01:17.813 --> 00:01:21.073
Perhaps the phage itself makes resistance after contact.

19
00:01:22.333 --> 00:01:27.393
Then each exposed cell gets a similar small chance, independent of its family

20
00:01:27.393 --> 00:01:32.953
history. The competing models therefore predict different shapes across cultures.

21
00:01:34.117 --> 00:01:38.577
If variants arise during growth, what pattern should appear after exposure?

22
00:01:40.017 --> 00:01:44.377
Similar survivor counts, or uneven counts with jackpots and empty plates?

23
00:01:45.617 --> 00:01:48.657
Use the family trees, not the final plates, to decide.

24
00:01:54.650 --> 00:01:55.970
Now open the gate.

25
00:01:57.490 --> 00:01:59.390
Phage removes susceptible blue cells.

26
00:02:00.750 --> 00:02:03.310
Resistant yellow cells remain and form colonies.

27
00:02:05.090 --> 00:02:09.250
Exposure changes which variants are visible, not their earlier family histories.

28
00:02:10.650 --> 00:02:13.390
The phage acts as a filter applied after growth.

29
00:02:14.433 --> 00:02:19.913
The growth model predicts this shape: several empty cultures, some small counts, and

30
00:02:19.913 --> 00:02:21.093
an occasional jackpot.

31
00:02:22.493 --> 00:02:25.493
Contact-induced resistance predicts much tighter scatter.

32
00:02:26.973 --> 00:02:29.873
Averages alone can hide this crucial difference in shape.

33
00:02:30.917 --> 00:02:36.877
Luria and Delbrück compared many independent cultures with repeated samples from one

34
00:02:36.877 --> 00:02:43.397
bulk culture. Independent cultures scattered far beyond ordinary sampling and

35
00:02:43.397 --> 00:02:44.397
plating variation.

36
00:02:45.837 --> 00:02:50.057
Repeated bulk samples reveal how much scatter handling alone can produce.

37
00:02:50.057 --> 00:02:52.657
The contrast tests timing.

38
00:02:53.750 --> 00:02:55.470
This result has a boundary.

39
00:02:56.790 --> 00:03:01.550
It shows that phage resistance arose before phage exposure in this system.

40
00:03:02.890 --> 00:03:06.170
Mutation rates can still change with cellular conditions.

41
00:03:07.510 --> 00:03:11.170
Changing a rate is not the same as directing a useful mutation.

42
00:03:12.333 --> 00:03:18.253
Mutation creates heritable DNA variants; selection changes how common they become.

43
00:03:19.513 --> 00:03:21.353
The sequence change starts a branch.

44
00:03:22.613 --> 00:03:26.153
The environment changes the branch's representation afterward.

45
00:03:27.533 --> 00:03:31.413
Keep origin and sorting separate whenever you interpret adaptation.
